    labTitle: "OS Troubleshooting and Performance",
    practicalTasks: [
      { topic: "Solve system start up issues using Safe Mode: Boot into Safe Mode, identify problematic start up items, disable them using msconfig.", detail: "" },
      { topic: "Use Automatic Repair: Trigger the Advanced Startup menu, run Startup Repair, document the result.", detail: "" },
      { topic: "Create a System Restore point, then simulate a problem (uninstall a program), and rollback using System Restore.", detail: "" },
      { topic: "Check and solve performance issues using Task Manager (identify high CPU/memory processes), Resource Monitor (find disk-heavy processes), Disk Cleanup, and malware scan.", detail: "" },
      { topic: "Check Disk Health: Run wmic diskdrive get status, check Event Viewer for disk errors.", detail: "" },
      { topic: "Document the concept of BSOD: Create a reference sheet with 5 common error codes, their meanings, causes, and solutions.", detail: "" },
    ],
    viva: [
      { number: 1, question: "What is the difference between System Restore and System Reset?", answer: "System Restore rolls back system files, registry settings, and installed drivers to a previous restore point WITHOUT affecting personal files (documents, photos, downloads). It requires a previously created restore point. System Reset (now called 'Reset this PC') reinstalls Windows from scratch — it can either keep personal files (removes apps and settings) or remove everything (factory reset). System Reset does not require a restore point but takes longer. Use System Restore for recent problems (last few days); use System Reset when the system is severely corrupted or infected with malware that cannot be removed." },
      { number: 2, question: "What does the command 'sfc /scannow' do and when should you use it?", answer: "'sfc' stands for System File Checker. 'sfc /scannow' scans all protected Windows system files and replaces corrupted or modified files with correct versions from the Windows component store. Use it when: (1) You suspect system file corruption (frequent crashes, features not working), (2) After malware removal to repair any damaged system files, (3) Before major updates to ensure system integrity, (4) As part of BSOD troubleshooting. Run from Administrator Command Prompt. Takes 10-30 minutes. If SFC finds errors it cannot fix, follow with DISM: 'DISM /Online /Cleanup-Image /RestoreHealth' which repairs the component store itself, then run SFC again." },
      { number: 3, question: "A PC shows 100% disk usage constantly. Name three possible causes and their solutions.", answer: "Cause 1: Windows Search indexing service running aggressively. Solution: Open Services (services.msc), find Windows Search, set Startup type to Disabled, restart. Cause 2: Superfetch (SysMain) service preloading applications excessively. Solution: In Services, find SysMain, disable it. Cause 3: Antivirus doing a full system scan. Solution: Check your antivirus schedule, change scan time to after lab hours. Cause 4: Failing hard drive with read/write retries. Solution: Run CrystalDiskInfo or 'wmic diskdrive get status' — if status shows caution, backup data immediately and replace the drive." },
      { number: 4, question: "What information should you note when you see a BSOD?", answer: "When a BSOD occurs, immediately note: (1) The STOP error code (e.g., CRITICAL_PROCESS_DIED, 0x000000EF). (2) The file name mentioned (e.g., nvlddmkm.sys indicates NVIDIA graphics driver, atikmpag.sys indicates AMD graphics driver). (3) The exact time it happened. (4) What you were doing just before the BSOD (installing software, playing video, idle). (5) Whether it happens consistently or randomly. This information is crucial for diagnosis — the error code tells you the category of problem, the file name often identifies the faulty driver, and the context tells you whether it's triggered by specific software or hardware." },
      { number: 5, question: "What is the DISM command and when is it used compared to SFC?", answer: "DISM (Deployment Image Servicing and Management) is used to repair the Windows system image (the source repository from which SFC copies clean files). Use DISM when SFC reports that it found corruption but was unable to fix it — this means the source files in the component store are also corrupted. The command 'DISM /Online /Cleanup-Image /RestoreHealth' downloads clean system files from Windows Update and replaces corrupted ones in the component store. After DISM completes successfully, run 'sfc /scannow' again to fix the actual system files. DISM requires internet connection for /RestoreHealth unless you specify a local Windows image source with /Source parameter." },
    ],
