    viva: [
      { number: 1, question: "What is the difference between a static IP address and a dynamic IP address?", answer: "A static IP address is manually configured and remains constant across reboots. It's used for servers, network printers, and devices that need a predictable address for remote access. A dynamic IP address is automatically assigned by a DHCP server (usually the router) and may change each time the device connects. Dynamic IPs are easier to manage in large networks because the DHCP server handles allocation automatically, preventing duplicate IP conflicts. In our lab, we use static IPs for all PCs (192.168.1.101-120) so we always know which IP belongs to which computer. At home, DHCP is preferred for simplicity." },
      { number: 2, question: "What does the subnet mask 255.255.255.0 tell us?", answer: "he subnet mask 255.255.255.0 (written as /24 in CIDR notation) tells us that the first 24 bits (3 octets) of the IP address represent the network portion, and the last 8 bits (1 octet) represent the host portion. In practical terms: all devices with IP addresses starting with 192.168.1.x are on the same local network and can communicate directly. To reach devices outside this range (different network), traffic must go through the default gateway (router). With this mask, the network can have up to 254 host addresses (192.168.1.1 through 192.168.1.254), where .1 is typically the gateway and .255 is the broadcast address." },
      { number: 3, question: "A PC gets IP 169.254.x.x. What does this indicate and how do you fix it?", answer: "An IP address starting with 169.254 is an APIPA (Automatic Private IP Addressing) address. This means the PC failed to obtain an IP address from the DHCP server and automatically assigned itself a link-local address. This indicates: (1) The DHCP server (router) is not responding, (2) The network cable is disconnected or faulty, (3) The network adapter is disabled or malfunctioning, (4) There is a switch or router failure. To fix: Check physical cable connection and LED indicators; Run 'ipconfig /release' followed by 'ipconfig /renew'; Restart the network adapter; Check if DHCP service is running on the router; Try a static IP configuration temporarily; Test the cable with a cable tester or by swapping with a known-good cable." },
      { number: 4, question: "What is DNS and why is it necessary? What happens if DNS stops working?", answer: "DNS (Domain Name System) is a distributed database that translates human-readable domain names (like www.google.com) into machine-readable IP addresses (like 142.250.77.142). It's necessary because humans remember names easily but computers communicate using numbers. DNS works hierarchically: when you type a URL, your PC first checks its local cache, then queries the configured DNS server, which either knows the answer or queries upstream servers until the IP is found. If DNS stops working: you cannot access any website by name (typing www.google.com fails), but direct IP access still works (typing 142.250.77.142 in the browser address bar succeeds), and all other internet services using IP addresses continue working. This is a common diagnostic: if ping to 8.8.8.8 works but ping to www.google.com fails, the problem is DNS." },
      { number: 5, question: "What is the difference between Windows Firewall and an antivirus program?", answer: "Windows Firewall is a network security tool that monitors and controls incoming and outgoing network traffic based on rules. It acts as a barrier between your computer and the network, blocking unauthorized access attempts and controlling which applications can communicate over the network. An antivirus program (like Windows Defender) is a file security tool that scans files and memory for malicious software (viruses, trojans, ransomware), removes detected threats, and provides real-time protection against malware execution. They serve different purposes: the firewall protects against network-based attacks (hackers trying to connect to your PC), while antivirus protects against file-based threats (malicious software you might download or receive). Both are essential and should be enabled at all times." },
    ],
